the second year of destiny - really was  a high point for the franchise Bungie  shifted its approach to adding on to the  game from a series of larger expansions  to more constant smaller content updates  while telling a larger story that  unfolded over the course of the whole  year  the forsaken expansion and the year that  followed it we're the best bungees live  shooter has ever been destiny - latest  expansion shadow keep continues to build  on that foundation in just about every  way while the new content hitting the  game is a lot of fun it's the smaller  improvements to the way destiny two  plays that have the greatest impact  shadow keep sends destiny - in a new  direction and it's clearly just the  first step in a much longer journey  bungees new approach to the game is to  create a year long story arc through  seasonal content and activities but as a  result parts of shadow keep can feel a  bit thin as we wait for things to  continue to unfold that's primarily true  of the story campaign which centers on  destiny one character Eris morn  and sees us returning to the moon for  the first time since before destiny -  launched Eris has learned that the hive  Destiny's semi magical death worshiping  alien faction has discovered something  sinister there and it's conjuring up  deadly phantasms of your previous foes  so in a way shadow keep is a big reunion  tour of old destiny content Eris is back  you return to the d1 location of the  Moon and you fight slightly watered-down  versions of big bad guys you've  previously defeated such as dark below  raid boss crota and destiny - vanilla  boss Gul since we haven't been back to  the moon for two whole years it's  something of an amped-up  nostalgia trip but we're still waiting  to reach the long-term end game content  that will wrap up some of these story  threads destiny - story campaigns are  often a bit quick and lackluster there  are some exciting missions particularly  as you band together with other  Guardians and tanks to raid the new  scarlet keep location but part of the  issue is that this is the prologue of a  larger story being told over the course  of the year and that can make it a bit  unsatisfying you'll probably be a bit  surprised when it's suddenly over after  4 or 5 hours of dedicated play but  revisiting the Moon is pretty cool  especially because Bungie has amped up  the location spookiness factor lunar  tunnels are filled with frightening  screams of hidden terrors there are  plenty of tough enemies to dispatch in  the  whole place carries an air of haunted  mystery it seemed so far we've only  scratched the surface of what's hidden  on the moon destiny is at its best when  it's full of secrets for the community  to discover and the game has already  provided a few big baffling mysteries  that have required everyone banding  together to work out the rest of the  expansion's new content is engaging as  well with shadow keep providing a  variety of experiences no matter what  kind of player you might be the new  nightmare hunts are pretty much mini  strikes making them quick palatable boss  fights that help you grab new gear a new  take on nightfall strikes the tougher  version of destiny 2's three players  strike activities are enticing thanks to  a ramping difficulty system that gives  you a challenge at a variety of power  levels exploring the moon has a lot to  offer as well though a lot of the  location is made up of old areas they're  deep and maze-like and every trip into  their depths feels dangerous  shadow keeps raid garden of salvation is  the most inventive of the expansions  content which is usually the case with  destiny's rates it represents pretty  much the toughest challenge in the game  and it's strange unique mechanics  require a great deal of coordination and  communication from your six player team  my team didn't finish it on the first  day but we had a blast working through  its various tough encounters as usual  destiny twos latest raid provides a  great incentive to keep pushing on the  gear grind bungees first bit of seasonal  content for shadow keep vex offensive  helps have more things to do for players  who burn through the expansion's base  content the six player activity has you  fighting off waves of enemies and it  takes after the menagerie activity from  last season it's a bit like a light  version of a raid activity requiring a  little more coordination than usual  without the punishing difficulty and  it's huge dump of rewards mean chasing  the best rolls for its many guns doesn't  feel like a chore next activity settling  down that ought to slow their progress  considerably great work  the content on offer and shadow keep is  fun to work through but the real  standout of the expansion is the way it  improves some of the underlying systems  of destiny 2's gameplay the big focus is  on pushing you to think about the way  you load out your character thanks to  the new armor 2.0 system previously in  destiny 2 armor had specific random  perks added to it which meant if you  wanted to focus on different kinds of  gameplay you had to switch to specific  gear but you spent so much time  switching between different pieces of  gear to push up your character's overall  power level that more nuanced stats like  how fast your grenades were charged or  how quickly you moved were easily  ignored shadow key changes that system  by making the perks movable between  pieces of gear with mods and unlike in  the past those mods are no longer  consumed on use that gives you more  chances to experiment with your loadout  to maximize the stats that give you the  kind of play style you're looking for or  help you excel in a specific role on a  team  it gives destiny to something of a more  RPG like focus and it encourages you to  spend more time thinking about how you  load out your character instead of just  equipping the items with the highest  numbers there are also big changes to  the leveling grind and shadow keep that  improve the game experience the early  climb up to the game's soft cap of 900  power is quick and relatively painless  with just about every piece of gear you  earn while playing improving on your  character once you hit the soft cap  things do slow down some as you have to  do tougher activities to get better gear  but shadow keep includes improvements to  keep you from getting bogged down the  new season past system and seasonal  artifact continue to give you rewards  based on experience you earn you get new  gear and cosmetic drops from the season  pass and additional power from the  seasonal artifact no matter what content  you're playing those two systems help  make the game feel more rewarding and  keep you from getting stalled as you try  to become powerful enough for garden of  salvation and other top-tier content it  also helps that between shadow keeps  activities on the moon the vex offensive  the raid and destiny choose usual  activities like strikes on the crucible  there's a variety of things to do to  keep progressing  shadow keep brings a big shift in the  fundamentals of destiny - and that has  only improved the game returning to the  moon is a spooky good time and while  shadow keep might not be as huge as  forsaken it still brings impressive  additions to the world that will take  time to fully explore but it's the  smaller more meaningful gameplay choices  that shadow ki provides that have me  wanting to jump back in and keep taking  on the vex and the hive  these are changes that destiny has  needed for a long time and they  represent a giant leap forward for  destiny -  [Music]
 

